@@ -0,0 +1,53 @@
+---
+id: dl-003-json-patch-explicit-move-prefix-check-patch-values-cloned-on-insert
+type: decision-log
+title: "JSON Patch: explicit move prefix check, patch values cloned on insert"
+status: pending
+---
+
+## Context
+
+A conformance review of `applyPatch` (task-003) found two places where the implementation relied
+on incidental behaviour of mutable JavaScript objects/arrays instead of directly encoding what RFC
+6902 requires, which let it diverge from the standard in edge cases:
+
+1. RFC 6902 section 4.4 requires that a `move`'s `from` location is not a proper prefix of its
+   `path` location (a location cannot be moved into one of its own children). The previous
+   implementation applied `move` as a literal "remove" followed by "add" and trusted the "add" to
+   fail on its own, because the removed location's parent would no longer exist. This happens to
+   hold for objects (removing a key leaves no trace of it), but not for arrays: removing an element
+   shifts later elements down by one index, so the "add" can coincidentally find a real, unrelated
+   element at the same index and succeed instead of failing.
+2. dl-002 decided that `copy` must deep-clone the value it reads from the document, so a copy never
+   shares structure with its source and a later operation on one cannot silently mutate the other.
+   The same hazard exists for `add` and `replace`, just with a different source: their `value` comes
+   from the caller's `patch` argument, not from the document. Without cloning, a later operation in
+   the same patch that mutates an inserted value also mutates the object embedded in the caller's
+   `patch` array, which `applyPatch`'s contract never promised to leave alone but which is just as
+   surprising as the `copy` case dl-002 already ruled on.
+
+## Decision
+
+- `applyMove` now checks, before removing anything, whether `from`'s JSON Pointer reference tokens
+  are a *proper* prefix of `path`'s (strictly shorter, and equal token-for-token over that shorter
+  length). If so, it throws immediately. Equal pointers (`from === path`, a no-op move) and the
+  reverse direction (moving a descendant up to replace one of its ancestors) are both still
+  allowed, since neither is forbidden by section 4.4.
+- `validateOperation` now deep-clones `value` for `add`, `replace` and `test` operations when it
+  builds the validated `JsonPatchOperation`, before any `apply*` function sees it. This is the only
+  place an operation's `value` is read out of the caller-supplied `patch`, so a single clone there
+  guarantees every value `applyAdd`/`applyReplace` ever inserts into the document is independent of
+  the `patch` argument. `applyCopy` keeps its own `deepClone` call from dl-002, since it clones a
+  value read from the *document* (a different aliasing hazard, unrelated to the patch argument).
+
+## Consequences
+
+- A `move` whose `from` is a proper prefix of `path` always throws, regardless of container type,
+  matching RFC 6902 section 4.4 exactly instead of only in the cases where it happened to fail on
+  its own.
+- Callers can mutate an `applyPatch` result freely without risk of also mutating objects still
+  referenced by the `patch` array they passed in, for every operation that inserts a value
+  (`add`, `replace`, `move`, `copy`), not only `copy`.
+- `test` operations now also clone `value` on parse, even though they never insert it into the
+  document; this is a negligible cost and keeps `validateOperation`'s three `value`-bearing cases
+  (`add`, `replace`, `test`) uniform rather than special-casing `test`.
