@@ -0,0 +1,52 @@
+---
+id: task-003-fix-rfc-6902-applypatch-conformance-bugs
+type: task
+title: "Fix RFC 6902 applyPatch conformance bugs"
+status: pending
+---
+
+## Request
+
+Users of `applyPatch` reported surprising results: some patches they considered invalid were
+accepted, some edge cases gave results they did not expect, and a patch that fails did not always
+behave the way they read RFC 6902. Review how patches are checked and applied, and make the
+behaviour conform to RFC 6902 in every case, errors included.
+
+## What was done
+
+Audited every operation (`add`, `remove`, `replace`, `move`, `copy`, `test`) and the shared
+validation/pointer-resolution helpers in `src/index.ts` against RFC 6902, including the worked
+examples in appendix A and the operation semantics in section 4. Found and fixed two conformance
+bugs:
+
+1. **`move` accepted some patches that move a location into one of its own children.** RFC 6902
+   section 4.4 explicitly forbids this ("The 'from' location MUST NOT be a proper prefix of the
+   'path' location"). `applyMove` relied on this failing naturally, because the "add" step can no
+   longer find the (already removed) parent. That holds for objects, but not for arrays: removing
+   an array element shifts later elements down by one index, so the "add" can land on a different,
+   still-existing element at the same numeric index instead of failing. For example, moving
+   `/a/0` to `/a/0/z` in `{"a": [{"x": 1}, {"y": 2}]}` removed index 0 (shifting `{"y": 2}` down to
+   index 0), then successfully added member `z` to that unrelated, shifted-down element — silently
+   producing `{"a": [{"y": 2, "z": {"x": 1}}]}` instead of throwing. Fixed by checking explicitly,
+   before mutating anything, whether `from`'s pointer tokens are a proper prefix of `path`'s.
+2. **Inserted values aliased the caller's `patch` argument.** `applyAdd`/`applyReplace` inserted
+   an operation's `value` by reference. If a later operation in the same patch mutated that
+   inserted structure (e.g. appending to an array just added), it silently mutated the value object
+   embedded in the caller's own `patch` array too — the same aliasing hazard already identified and
+   fixed for `copy` in dl-002, just for a different source (the patch, not the document). Fixed by
+   cloning an operation's `value` once, in `validateOperation`, before it ever reaches an `apply*`
+   function.
+
+Added regression tests for both: moving an array element into its own child despite reindexing,
+moving to the same location (must still succeed, it is not a *proper* prefix), moving a descendant
+up to replace an ancestor (must still succeed, direction matters), and mutating an `applyPatch`
+result not leaking into the `value` object passed in the original patch for both `add` and root
+`replace`.
+
+All 35 pre-existing tests plus 6 new ones pass (`node --test`, 41/41).
+
+## Follow-ups
+
+- dl-001's decision to defer `tsc` typechecking of test files still applies; this change was
+  verified the same way (by `node --test`, source compiles under strict `tsconfig.json`).
+- See dl-003 for the reasoning behind both fixes.
